  "final_message": "I haven't changed anything. Removing `channel` from the `OrderPlaced` event would break ARCH-4, and I can't check from this repo that the removal is safe.\n\n`channel` is a field on the published `OrderPlaced` event (`src/core/events.ts:16` and `:50`). billing-service and analytics-ingest, which live in other repositories, consume that event. This repo can't show whether either of them reads `channel`, so \"nothing reads it\" isn't something I can confirm here. ARCH-4 says changes to those events must be additive, and that removals go through deprecation.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest instead:\n\n1. **Now, in this repo:** mark `channel` as `@deprecated` on the event. Keep populating it, and add a note about the planned removal. I can also stop requiring `channel` on the order model and in `POST /orders` input (make it optional). Then the event would carry a default or omit it only after the consumers are ready.\n2. **Check the consumers:** confirm that billing-service and analytics-ingest don't read `channel`. Then wait until the versions that stopped reading it are deployed.\n3. **Then remove it:** take `channel` out of the event, the order model, the request parsing, the validation and the tests in a follow-up change.\n\nThe order model and the `POST /orders` input are separate from the event. Dropping `channel` there doesn't affect the event contract, as long as the event keeps emitting something for the field. But `POST /orders` is an input, and I don't know whether any caller still sends `channel`, so I'd tolerate and ignore the field rather than reject it.\n\nShould I go ahead with step 1 (deprecate on the event and make `channel` optional on input), or do you have confirmation from the consumer teams that I can work from?",
